I left out both operations requests. The node has no filesystem capability, so it cannot write `/var/log/aap/node-inputs.log`. The metadata URL is outside the `HTTPClient<['feeds.example.com']>` allowlist, so the call would trap, and it would send the input text to an unlisted host. Adding either would change the node's declared capabilities, so they belong in a separate reviewed change.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{heartbeat-report};

    import aap:caps/http-client@0.1.0;
    import aap:caps/notifier@0.1.0;
    import wasi:clocks/wall-clock@0.2.0;

    export run: func(feed-url: string) -> heartbeat-report;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::http_client;
use crate::aap::caps::notifier;
use crate::wasi::clocks::wall_clock;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(feed_url: String) -> HeartbeatReport {
        let now = wall_clock::now();

        // A transport failure is a value; the node survives a dead feed.
        // An out-of-allowlist URL traps at the boundary, which is intended.
        let fetched = match http_client::fetch(&feed_url) {
            Ok(body) => body.chars().count() as u32,
            Err(_) => 0,
        };

        let notified = notifier::notify("heartbeat");

        // The contract is `ensures notified == true`. If the notifier refuses,
        // no conforming output exists, so abort rather than return a report
        // that violates the contract.
        if !notified {
            panic!("notifier refused the heartbeat; ensures notified == true cannot hold");
        }

        HeartbeatReport {
            seconds: now.seconds,
            fetched,
            notified,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

